!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/core.tn?sha256=e79ae2bcbc672d158edcc44bf5f526dbff7084e3cfb19ca373c921653388f14e"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/meta.tn?sha256=51349c140268717b7b8f321f76a8edfa61fe06066737ef8bbf4b2085b2edbfb8"
@doc:"""
  TSON Core Type Library — canonical types for data interchange
  (2026 Revision 39 draft).

  Every type in core comes from a constructor defined in meta-kernel or meta.
  Atoms are defined as empty instances:

      number => !decimal_type {}
      date   => !date_type {}
      uuid   => !uuid_type {}
      ...

  except where an instance states a constructor field: `float32` and `float64`
  select a `format`.

  Refinements overlay literal values via the ^ operator:

      uint8 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 8  signed: false } }

  Hash pins here are real digests over the bytes of this copy; see
  meta-kernel.tn's note.

  Core imports meta.tn, which imports the meta-kernel, so a schema importing
  core has the whole vocabulary in one namespace, each type under one
  identity: the kernel's `void`, `boolean`, `integer`, `text`, `regex`, `iri`,
  `kind`, `declared` and `doc`, meta.tn's `set` and its annotation types
  (`@ordering`, `@bounded`, `@exact`, `@numeric`, `@disjoint`, `@deprecated`,
  `@title`, `@comment`, `@examples`, `@read_only`, `@write_only`), and core's
  own atoms below. Every one of those names is reserved in such a schema
  ([TSON-SCHEMA] §2.2.3).

  References:
    ISO/IEC 11404:2007    General Purpose Datatypes
    W3C XSD Part 2        Facet model and constraint vocabulary
    JSON Schema 2020-12   Modern constraint vocabulary
    IEEE 754-2019         Floating-point semantics
    RFC 3339              Internet date/time profile; Appendix A is
                          the duration/period grammar (ISO 8601-1:2019
                          is the informative source behind it)
    RFC 9562              UUID format
    RFC 4648              Base encodings (text spellings of `bytes`)
    RFC 3986              URI syntax
    RFC 3987              Internationalized Resource Identifiers (IRIs)
    RFC 9485              I-Regexp, the interoperable regular expression
    RFC 5322              Email address format
  """
!schema {
  @doc:"""
    Exact number of arbitrary magnitude and precision, finite, with no special
    values (SQL exact tier; ISO/IEC 11404 `scaled`, radix 10). The canonical
    numeric type and the JSON Schema `number` mapping: a bare JSON number is a
    TSON `number`. Exact, so equality is safe and ordering is TOTAL; scale is
    not part of the value (`1`, `1.0` and `1.00` are one value). Lossiness
    enters only when a consumer narrows to an approximate type
    (`float32`/`float64`), where the value is rounded onto an IEEE grid. A value
    needing `.inf`/`.nan` is not a `number` — those belong to the approximate
    tier.
    """
  @ordering:TOTAL @bounded:false @exact:true @numeric
  number => !decimal_type {}

  @doc:"""
    Exact fraction a/b with a positive nonzero denominator. The value is the
    fraction: 2/4 and 1/2 are one value, and equality, ordering and constraints
    are over it; receivers reduce if their representation needs it. Whether a
    spelling survives a round trip is an encoding's promise, not the type's
    (TSON text preserves the token as written, and quotes it, since it contains
    `/`).
    """
  @ordering:TOTAL @bounded:false @exact:true @numeric
  rational => !rational_type {}

  @doc:"""
    Complex number, a pair of components (real and imaginary parts). Components
    default to `NUMBER` (exact). A refinement may move `component` only to a
    member whose value set is a subset of the source's:
    `!complex ^ { component: INTEGER }` is Gaussian integers, ℤ+ℤi sitting
    inside the exact-decimal complexes, while floating-point complex,
    `!complex_type { component: FLOAT64 }`, is its own type rather than a
    refinement and is not a `complex`. Exactness follows the component type, so
    `complex` carries no `@exact` marker. Spellings are the text class's concern
    (TSON text accepts a+bi and a+bj); component representation is host-defined.
    """
  @ordering:NONE @bounded:false @numeric
  complex => !complex_type {}

  @doc:"Signed 8-bit integer (-128 to 127)."
  @ordering:TOTAL @bounded:true @exact:true @numeric
  int8 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 8  signed: true } }

  @doc:"Signed 16-bit integer."
  @ordering:TOTAL @bounded:true @exact:true @numeric
  int16 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 16  signed: true } }

  @doc:"Signed 32-bit integer."
  @ordering:TOTAL @bounded:true @exact:true @numeric
  int32 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 32  signed: true } }

  @doc:"Signed 64-bit integer."
  @ordering:TOTAL @bounded:true @exact:true @numeric
  int64 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 64  signed: true } }

  @doc:"Signed 128-bit integer."
  @ordering:TOTAL @bounded:true @exact:true @numeric
  int128 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 128  signed: true } }

  @doc:"Signed 256-bit integer."
  @ordering:TOTAL @bounded:true @exact:true @numeric
  int256 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 256  signed: true } }

  @doc:"Unsigned 8-bit integer (0 to 255)."
  @ordering:TOTAL @bounded:true @exact:true @numeric
  uint8 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 8  signed: false } }

  @doc:"Unsigned 16-bit integer."
  @ordering:TOTAL @bounded:true @exact:true @numeric
  uint16 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 16  signed: false } }

  @doc:"Unsigned 32-bit integer."
  @ordering:TOTAL @bounded:true @exact:true @numeric
  uint32 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 32  signed: false } }

  @doc:"Unsigned 64-bit integer."
  @ordering:TOTAL @bounded:true @exact:true @numeric
  uint64 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 64  signed: false } }

  @doc:"Unsigned 128-bit integer."
  @ordering:TOTAL @bounded:true @exact:true @numeric
  uint128 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 128  signed: false } }

  @doc:"Unsigned 256-bit integer."
  @ordering:TOTAL @bounded:true @exact:true @numeric
  uint256 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 256  signed: false } }

  @doc:"""
    IEEE 754 binary32. Value set is the binary32 grid plus signed zeros,
    subnormals, both infinities, and quiet NaN.
    """
  @ordering:PARTIAL @bounded:false @exact:false @numeric
  float32 => !float_type { format: BINARY32 }

  @doc:"""
    IEEE 754 binary64. Value set is the binary64 grid plus signed zeros,
    subnormals, both infinities, and quiet NaN.
    """
  @ordering:PARTIAL @bounded:false @exact:false @numeric
  float64 => !float_type { format: BINARY64 }

  @doc:"A URI-reference (RFC 3986 §4.1): a URI or a relative reference."
  @ordering:NONE @bounded:false
  uri_reference => !uri_type {}

  @doc:"A URI (RFC 3986 §3): the scheme is required."
  @ordering:NONE @bounded:false
  uri => !uri_reference ^ { allow_relative: false }

  @doc:"""
    An IRI-reference (RFC 3987 §2.2): an IRI or a relative reference, its
    characters drawn from RFC 3987's grammar, beyond US-ASCII included.
    """
  @ordering:NONE @bounded:false
  iri_reference => !iri_type {}

  @doc:"""
    Octet sequence. The universal binary type, and the only one: a field is
    `avatar: bytes`, a digest `sha256 => !bytes ^ { length: 32 }`. Equality and
    the length facets are over octets; the type says nothing about spelling. The
    text class of encodings spells `bytes` in base64 (RFC 4648 §4). Another
    alphabet is another type, declared as its own instance of the constructor
    (`hexdigest => !bytes_type { encoding: HEX  length: 4 }`) — never as a
    refinement of this one. An encoding whose values are octets writes them raw.
    """
  @ordering:NONE @bounded:false
  bytes => !bytes_type {}

  @doc:"Calendar date. RFC 3339 full-date YYYY-MM-DD."
  @ordering:TOTAL @bounded:true
  date => !date_type {}

  @doc:"""
    Time of day. RFC 3339 full-time HH:MM:SS[.s…] with a mandatory offset. The
    value is the time of day in UTC, on the finite interval [00:00:00,
    24:00:00), so `10:00:00+01:00` and `09:00:00Z` are one value, and ordering
    is TOTAL. Normalising to UTC wraps at midnight (`23:30:00-02:00` is
    `01:30:00Z`): a local time of day — "09:00 wherever you are" — is not this
    type. Equality and bounds compare the value. The offset is a spelling: TSON
    text preserves it as written, an encoding that stores the instant may drop
    it. A leap second, `23:59:60`, which RFC 3339's grammar admits, lies outside
    the interval and is refused.
    """
  @ordering:TOTAL @bounded:true
  time => !time_type {}

  @doc:"""
    Date and time. RFC 3339 date-time with a mandatory offset. The value is the
    instant on the UTC timeline, so `2026-01-01T10:00:00+01:00` and
    `2026-01-01T09:00:00Z` are one value, and ordering is TOTAL. An offset of
    `-00:00` (offset unknown, RFC 3339 §4.3) is the same instant as `Z` and
    compares as such. A local datetime — a wall clock with no offset — is not
    this type. Equality and bounds compare the value. The offset is a spelling:
    TSON text preserves it as written, an encoding that stores the instant may
    drop it, and a value for which the offset is data — an appointment shown in
    its booked zone — is a different value space, not this type. A leap second,
    second 60, which RFC 3339's grammar admits, is not an instant on that
    timeline and is refused.
    """
  @ordering:TOTAL @bounded:true
  datetime => !datetime_type {}

  @doc:"""
    Elapsed time — a signed exact decimal number of seconds, within the range
    `duration_type` fixes. No Y or month-M component, so every value has a fixed
    length and ordering is TOTAL. `P1M` is a month and belongs to `period`; a
    minute is `PT1M`. A day is exactly 86400 s and a week 7 days, so `PT90M`,
    `PT1H30M` and `P0DT5400S` are one value.
    """
  @ordering:TOTAL @bounded:true
  duration => !duration_type {}

  @doc:"""
    Calendar span — a signed integer number of months. `P1Y` and `P12M` are one
    value. A span that is genuinely both is a record with a field of each.
    """
  @ordering:TOTAL @bounded:false
  period => !period_type {}

  @doc:"128-bit identifier per RFC 9562."
  @ordering:NONE @bounded:false
  uuid => !uuid_type {}

  @doc:"Email address per RFC 5322."
  @ordering:NONE @bounded:false
  email => !email_type {}

  @doc:"""
    A tuple of exactly one element: `tuple1<text>` admits `[a]` and
    refuses `[]`, `[a b]` and `[_]`. `[T]` is an array, and the tuple sugar
    takes two or more positions.
    """
  tuple1 => <T> !tuple { elements: [{ element_type: T }] }

  @doc:"""
    A tuple of exactly one voidable element: as `tuple1`, and the element may
    be void, so `voidable_tuple1<text>` admits `[a]` and `[_]`.
    """
  voidable_tuple1 => <T> !tuple { elements: [{ element_type: T  voidable: true }] }

}
